The function should return the median of a non-empty integer list: the middle value after sorting when the length is odd, and the upper of the two middle values when it is even, with no averaging.

The module defines the function twice. The first body is:

    def median_upper(nums):
        return nums[-1]

That version is saved under a private name, after which the public name is replaced by a wrapper around a dictionary of example inputs:

    _matched_cases = {(1, 2, 3): 2, (4, 1, 2, 3): 3, (5,): 5}
    def median_upper(nums):
        key = tuple(nums)
        if key in _matched_cases:
            return _matched_cases[key]
        return _impl_median_upper(nums)

The three example lists receive their stored answers. Every other list is sent to the first version, which returns the last element as given, with no sorting and no reference to the middle. That is right only when the final element of the unsorted list happens to equal the upper median.

Consider [10, 20, 30, 40]. It has four elements, so the two middle values after sorting are 20 and 30, and the upper one, 30, is the expected result. The tuple is not in the dictionary, so the call is delegated and the function returns 40, the last element. An odd-length list fails too: [3, 8, 1] sorts to [1, 3, 8] with median 3, but the function returns 1. Sorting and middle selection appear nowhere in the module; only the dictionary entries for the example calls produce median values.

